      "output": {
        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "All three tests are marked `{test}` for `requirements.layout.project`. Together they cover each obligation in the requirement.\n\n- **Markdown in `md/`:** `tests/repository.rs#fn[project_book_uses_the_repository_local_preprocessor_alias]` asserts that `book.toml` has `book.src == \"md\"`. It also asserts that `md/SUMMARY.md` exists.\n- **`habeas` crate source in `src/`:** `tests/repository.rs#fn[crate_targets_are_declared_in_src]` asserts that `Cargo.toml` declares the lib path `src/lib.rs`. It also asserts that there is exactly one bin, named `habeas`, at `src/main.rs`. The book test also checks that `src/lib.rs` and `src/main.rs` exist.\n- **`trust-but-verify` source in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/`:** `tests/repository.rs#fn[workspace_contains_frontend_and_sdk_but_not_the_vendor_fork]` asserts that `crates/trust-but-verify/src/lib.rs` exists. It also asserts that the `trust-but-verify` dependency path is `crates/trust-but-verify`. A `cargo metadata` check shows the workspace members are exactly `habeas` and `trust-but-verify`.\n- **The book uses the Habeas preprocessor as specified by `requirements.cli.mdbook`:** The book test asserts that the `preprocessor.habeas` command is `cargo habeas mdbook`. It also asserts that the renderers are `[\"habeas\"]` and that `.cargo/config.toml` has the alias `habeas = run --release -p habeas --`. This applies the cited requirement to the project's own book. The \"consumer books\" clause belongs to the cited requirement, so it needs no test here.\n\nI found no behavior that a plausible violation could slip past these assertions. For example, if `md/` or either crate's source were moved, the path and file-existence assertions would fail. If the preprocessor or alias were misconfigured, the command and alias assertions would fail."
